<Layout title="About">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    Hi, I'm Morgan! As a PhD candidate in the joint UC Berkeley/UCSF
    Bioengineering program, I get to spend my days doing something I
    genuinely love: working on incredible science while connecting with my
    community. As a researcher in Dr. Kevin Healy's lab, I engineer
    hyaluronic acid hydrogels that help the body regenerate tissue after
    complex soft tissue injuries like rotator cuff injuries. My
    interdisciplinary background in hydrogel engineering, biochemistry, and
    cell-material interactions lends itself to the complexity of this
    engineering challenge&mdash;designing a therapeutic that is effective,
    safe, and can be used by <em>anyone</em> regardless of biology or
    geography. As an inventor on MuscleMatrix, I've gotten to see that work
    move from the bench toward something that could reach the clinic and
    help patients directly.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before Berkeley, I got to explore a huge range of science, and each
    stop shaped how I approach research today. In the Directed Assembly of
    Particles and Suspensions lab, I fabricated dual-network hydrogels with
    magnetically responsive fiber reinforcement for bone grafts, ran COMSOL
    simulations to chase new toughening mechanisms, and built freeze-casted
    nerve repair conduits alongside collaborators at Dartmouth College and
    Harvard Medical School&mdash;work that taught me just how much
    materials science and mechanics matter for a device to actually survive
    inside the body. I also screened CRISPR-edited cell lines at Editas
    Medicine, chased druggable targets for cancer and Sickle Cell Disease
    at UConn Health and Vertex Pharmaceuticals, and worked hands-on with
    patients as an EMT and Clinical Care Technician. That last piece
    matters most to me: it's the reason I never think about a hydrogel as
    purely a materials problem. Somewhere at the other end is a patient,
    and that's who I'm actually designing for.
  </p>

  <p>
    That same belief carries into how I spend my time outside the lab. I
    volunteer weekly with Food Not Bombs East Bay, and I care deeply about
    mutual aid and the social and political forces that determine who
    actually gets access to science, medicine, and care. I'm also
    passionate about teaching, and about helping other people find and
    chase their own passions in STEM&mdash;whether that's a fellow
    researcher or someone who's never thought of themselves as "a science
    person" before. If that's you, I'd love to talk.
  </p>

  <h2>Education</h2>
  <p>
    PhD, Bioengineering &mdash; University of California, Berkeley (2021&ndash;present)<br />
    BS, Biochemistry &mdash; Northeastern University (2016&ndash;2021)
  </p>

